-27-02/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Stoa of Attalos is a stoa (a covered walkway or portico) in the Agora of Athens, Greece [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_of_Attalos]; It was built by King Attalos II of Pergamon [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_of_Attalos]; The stoa had two storeys [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_of_Attalos]; The stoa was in frequent use in antiquity until its woodwork was burned [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_of_Attalos]; The Heruli burned its woodwork [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_of_Attalos]; The burning happened in AD 267 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_of_Attalos]; Today the Stoa houses a museum (the building standing today is the 1952-56 reconstruction, which houses the Museum of the Ancient Agora) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoa_of_Attalos]"}]'::jsonb),
